= Summary and reflection

NTG-agent-vnext brings scoped knowledge retrieval, specialist delegation, configurable model access and interactive agent responses into a common platform. The inspected implementation provides evidence for these integrations. The retrieval trials add a concrete outcome: strong reported top-five coverage on the supplied pilot and unchanged hit rates in a warm-cache rerun, together with measurable differences in relative retrieval latency. The evidence supports these bounded findings while leaving human answer quality and several operational targets for subsequent validation.

From the team's perspective, the most consequential design lesson is that access boundaries must shape orchestration. Moving toward a dedicated knowledge context for each agent clarified what a specialist owns and how another agent may use its capability. The resulting delegation path has an understandable contract: authorise the call, let the inner agent retrieve within its own context, and return an answer. The remaining lesson is to test this contract explicitly, including denied and revoked access, rather than relying only on a successful demonstration.

Provider integration produced a related lesson about configuration. A model name or provider label does not fully describe which request options an endpoint accepts. The thinking probe turns part of that uncertainty into a concrete compatibility check. Its interpretation must remain precise: a successful probe validates the tested request, while quality and reliability require separate observations. Future work should preserve the tested configuration and failure reason so administrators can distinguish unsupported options from temporary connection problems.

Local environment setup was another practical episode. The team reported friction from collecting configuration values and preparing services, and introduced an interactive setup script with defaults for non-sensitive internal settings. The lesson is to treat developer onboarding as part of the deliverable. A documented, repeatable setup path helps future contributors understand the system, even though its time savings have not yet been measured. Independently repeating the fresh-machine checks reported in PR 285 would provide a direct next validation.

The skill work added a lesson about interactive correctness. PR 286 records changes to seeded input bindings, stable surface identifiers and client-capability handling. From the team's perspective, these issues show that a convincing generated answer and a usable workflow are different deliverables. Future acceptance criteria should include a complete user journey across several turns, including input edits and continuation, alongside checks of the generated content. @githubhistory

The evaluation experience shows why measurement preparation should begin alongside implementation. The supplied trial summaries are already useful for analysing coverage and latency tradeoffs, but absent raw records and unresolved metric wording limit what can be independently reconstructed. Human assessment was not completed within the available time. In a future iteration, the team would define the scoring unit, retain executable evaluation configurations and reserve time for judging outputs before the final reporting stage.

The final priorities are therefore consolidation and validation. The team should confirm outstanding delivery and contribution records, preserve the retrieval artefacts, evaluate answers and access boundaries, and test the remaining operational criteria under documented conditions. This would build on the implemented platform and the initial retrieval findings while producing stronger evidence for deployment and continued development.
